46. Budget Systems and Budgetary Policy in India
Paper: Public Finance (ECNCC303) | Unit: V | Topic: Classical and modern theories of budgeting, budget framing, incremental and zero-base budgeting, programme and performance budgeting, budget as an instrument of economic policy, budgetary policy in India
Introduction
A budget is the government's annual statement of estimated revenues and expenditures. It is the main tool of fiscal policy and a mechanism of accountability.
Classical and modern theories
• Classical: the budget should be balanced, small and focused on controlling expenditure; deficit is a sin.
• Modern (Keynesian): the budget is an instrument for stabilisation, growth and redistribution; deficits or surpluses are used according to the cycle. Functional finance (Lerner).
Budget framing in India
Under Article 112, the Union Budget (Annual Financial Statement) is presented to Parliament by the Finance Minister. Stages: preparation by the Ministry of Finance (budget circular, estimates from ministries), presentation, general discussion, voting on demands for grants, passage of the Appropriation Bill and Finance Bill. Components: Revenue and Capital Budget; Consolidated Fund, Contingency Fund, Public Account.
Types of budgeting
1. Incremental budgeting: the next year's budget adds a percentage to the previous year's allocation. Simple but perpetuates inefficiency.
2. Zero-base budgeting (ZBB): every item justified from scratch (Peter Pyhrr, Texas Instruments; US federal under Carter; India Dept of Science and Technology in 1983). Steps: decision units, decision packages, ranking. Benefits: efficiency; costs: paperwork and time.
3. Programme budgeting (PPBS): activities grouped into programmes with objectives, linking inputs to outputs and long-term plans (US Defence, McNamara).
4. Performance budgeting: emphasis on outputs and outcomes. India adopted it from 1969-70 and introduced the Outcome Budget (2005). Evaluate programmes by performance indicators.
Budget as an instrument of economic policy
• Allocation: public goods, infrastructure.
• Distribution: subsidies, transfers, progressive taxes.
• Stabilisation: deficit during recession and surplus in boom.
• Growth: capital expenditure, tax incentives.
• Gender, green and outcome budgets; fiscal discipline via the FRBM Act (2003) and amendments, with a glide path, escape clause (used during COVID).
Budgetary policy in India: an overview of Union Budgets
Early decades: high public investment, rising subsidies and deficits. The 1991 crisis led to fiscal consolidation: lower fiscal deficit, tax reform (rationalised rates, GST 2017), disinvestment. 2008-09 stimulus raised deficits. The COVID years (2020-21) saw deficits of about 9 per cent of GDP, followed by consolidation and an emphasis on capital expenditure (effective capital expenditure), infrastructure (PM Gati Shakti), and a medium-term path to reduce fiscal deficit. Challenges: subsidies, revenue mobilisation, off-budget borrowings, quality of expenditure.
Conclusion
Budgeting has evolved from a control tool to a policy instrument; Indian reforms aim at transparency, outcome orientation and fiscal sustainability.